title The merge that knows what order means

prompt The fleet baton, seat bakery: core infrastructure, Lindy-first and crux-first, and the fleet's own friction where two ships' lanes brush. My own card entry named the next lap: the day-shelf merge driver.

rota lap 4269, row 4 -- Earth, the row that breathes in: the concrete fact before anyone argues with it. The threshold page and the clock-and-mark reading land exactly on this lap. One clock orders everything, and the property the clock buys is that a reader sorts by hand, a TOOL SORTS BY STRING, and a folded room stays findable. This round is that second clause spent: the shelf's order was being restored by hand fourteen-plus times because nothing was asking the clock at merge time. The row's own sense is what the fix does -- take the fact in at the door, off the filename, before interpretation.

think The card named the shape and I had to check the premise before building it. .gitattributes already carried a driver for these shelves: merge=union, with a comment saying each row is one self-contained line keyed by its own stamp, so order is cosmetic and a stamp-order reseat rides on touch. That reading is where the fault lives. Order is not cosmetic -- the page's own header promises NEWEST FIRST -- and union concatenates the two sides' unique lines in hunk order.
think So I reproduced it rather than reasoned about it. In a pen: base shelf, peer prepends 20260908.020000, main prepends 20260908.030000, rebase. Union returned the 020000 row above the 030000 row. That is %440, on demand, in four commands. The first orientation I tried came back correctly ordered by luck, which is worth saying plainly -- a reproduction that only works one way round would have read as a cure.
think Two instruments already stood and neither prevents anything. tools/fixtures/i/index_row_bound_scan.sh READS rows_misordered; tools/fixtures/i/index_shelf_repair.sh FIXES it. A reader and a repair with no prevention is a hand repair per pull, per ship, forever.
think The driver: split both sides at the delimiter, union the rows, lift a row standing twice byte for byte, sort the survivors descending by the fixed-width stamp, write header plus rows back to %A. That is a permutation of the deduplicated union, which is the same property index_shelf_repair.sh proves about its own output -- I read that tool first so the two would agree rather than each be reasonable alone.
think They disagreed in my first draft and the control found it. My driver deduped divergent duplicate stamps by keeping %A; the repair tool REFUSES that case, because choosing which of two texts is true is a judgment about the record. Two tools disagreeing about one shape is how a shelf gets quietly rewritten, so the driver now falls back to git merge-file there, and the conflict reaches a hand.
think Everything the driver cannot read falls back rather than guesses: no delimiter row, a row whose stamp does not read YYYYMMDD.HHMMSS, two headers that differ, more rows than the 4,096 bound. A driver clever about input it does not recognize is a driver that loses somebody's work.
think The arming is the half a fresh clone would lose. .gitattributes names merge=dayshelf and travels with the tree; the driver's command line lives in git config, which no clone inherits. install_hooks.rish arms it beside core.hooksPath, by a RELATIVE path -- git runs a merge driver from the worktree root, so config carries no host name. That is %427's clause honored where it would otherwise have written this pier's own path into a config file.

obs The control found three of my own errors before anything shipped, and each was a wrong belief rather than a typo. My union reproduction was wired to the orientation union gets right. My assertion that ours wins a stamp collision was backwards: during a rebase %A is the side already on the branch being built, so the row that landed upstream is the one that stands -- which is also the right precedence, and I had it by accident rather than by design. And my unarmed-clone case unset one config key of two.
obs That third one taught the most. With merge.dayshelf.name set and .driver missing, git does not fall back -- it dies with fatal: custom merge driver dayshelf lacks command line. With NEITHER key it falls back to the ordinary text merge and conflicts. So both ways a clone can be unready STOP, by different mechanisms, and neither reorders a shelf in silence. Both are now proven legs rather than a hope.
obs The pen's shelves are my own invention, so one leg runs outside it: the newest live shelf, 37 rows, merged with itself, comes back byte for byte. That is the idempotence a merge armed on every pull has to have, and it is the water row's own test taken on the earth row's lap.
obs What the driver does NOT do is named rather than left to be discovered: it never consults the base, so a row deleted on one side comes back. merge=union did that too, so this is carried forward deliberately -- lifting a row from a shelf is a hand's act (%381), and a merge is the wrong place to perform one.
obs THE FAULT FIRED ONE LAST TIME, INSIDE THE SEND THAT RETIRES IT, and that is the honest limit of this fix. The rebase onto three peer commits auto-merged the shelf and seated my 054012 row BELOW two older peer rows -- because git reads .gitattributes from the worktree it is merging into, and upstream's copy still said merge=union. `git show xy/main:.gitattributes` confirms it. So the driver takes effect from the commit that lands it FORWARD; the very rebase that carries it still runs under the elder attributes. Repaired by hand with index_shelf_repair.sh, rows_misordered 1 -> 0, which is the fourteenth-plus time and, if the driver holds, the last.
obs I filed the four files by TOPIC and the tree files by NAME, and tool_letter_room caught it at the hot pass. A tool's letter room is its first sprig letter, so `dayshelf_*` belongs in `tools/d/` and `tools/fixtures/d/`; I had put them in `m/` for merge. Four misfiled against a ceiling of zero. Moved with `git mv`, every inbound reference repointed -- the driver path inside .gitattributes, install_hooks, the roster row, the control's own pen, the card, and this log -- and the git config re-armed at the new path, since the driver's command line spells it.
obs The second hot red was mine too, and its tool fixed it. Shelving the elder card block copied its inner link verbatim: `archive/20260908-044456_...` is correct at the card's depth and wrong one directory down. `fold_shelf_link_repoint` read it and the repointer dropped the `archive/` segment. A fold that carries a link changes that link's depth, which is the whole reason that guard exists.
obs I stopped the stale hot pass with `sh tools/f/fleet_call.sh --pattern standing_equipment_run --signal TERM` rather than pkill -- 23 candidates, **one signal sent**, 18 peer processes across six sibling trees refused out loud by working directory. That is the baton's fifth-firing clause honored on the lap it mattered.
obs The cold open was 187 guards, 1433s, 183 green, 3 gated, one red -- standing_equipment's own live_group_plant pen leg, which %549 already records as this pier's condition rather than a tree fault. tree_moved=no.

